"The Simple Science of Flight (https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262258470/the-simple-science-of-flight/)\nciteturn245view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262258470/the-simple-science-of-flight/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 96\nL0: 【0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: [Button: Close Menu ]\nL3: \nL4: 【1†Newsletter】\nL5: \nL6: SEARCH 【2†Image: Search】\nL7: \nL8: 【1†Newsletter】\nL9: \nL10: 【2†Image】\nL11: \nL12: [Input: Search]\nL13: \nL14: [Button: +]\nL15: \nL16: ### Books\nL17: \nL18: ### Authors\nL19: \nL20: ### On the site\nL21: \nL22: 【3†Image: Cover of 'The Simple Science of Flight' by Henk Tennekes †mit-press-new-us.imgix.net】 【4†Request exam copy†sampling.vitalsource.com】\nL23: \nL24: # The Simple Science of Flight\nL25: \nL26: ## From Insects to Jumbo Jets\nL27: \nL28: revised and expanded edition\nL29: \nL30: by 【5†Henk Tennekes】\nL31: ### eBook\nL32: \nL33: eBook\nL34: \nL35: ISBN: 9780262258470\nL36: \nL37: Pub date: September 4, 2009\nL38: \nL39: Publisher: The MIT Press\nL40: \nL41: 87 figures, 7 tables\nL42: \nL43: 【6†Penguin Random House†www.penguinrandomhouse.com】\nL44: \nL45: ### Paperback\nL46: ##### $29.95\nL47: \nL48: Paperback\nL49: \nL50: ISBN: 9780262513135\nL51: \nL52: Pub date: September 4, 2009\nL53: \nL54: Publisher: The MIT Press\nL55: \nL56: 87 figures, 7 tables\nL57: \nL58: 【7†MIT Press Bookstore†mitpressbookstore.mit.edu】【8†Penguin Random House†www.penguinrandomhouse.com】【9†Amazon†www.amazon.com】【10†Barnes and Noble†www.barnesandnoble.com】【11†Bookshop.org†bookshop.org】【12†Indiebound†www.indiebound.org】【13†Indigo†www.chapters.indigo.ca】\nL59: \nL60: 87 figures, 7 tables\nL61: \nL62:   * eBook\nL63:   * 9780262258470\nL64:   * Published: September 4th, 2009\nL65:   * Publisher: The MIT Press\nL66:   * Paperback\nL67:   * 9780262513135\nL68:   * Published: September 4th, 2009\nL69:   * Publisher: The MIT Press\nL70: \nL71: $29.95\nL72: \nL73:   * 【6†Penguin Random House†www.penguinrandomhouse.com】\nL74: \nL75: Other Retailers:\nL76: \nL77:   * 【6†Penguin Random House†www.penguinrandomhouse.com】\nL78: \nL79:   * 【14†Description】\nL80:   * 【15†Author(s)】\nL81:   * 【16†Resources】\nL82: \nL83: An investigation into how machines and living creatures fly, and of the similarities between butterflies and Boeings, paper airplanes and plovers.\nL84: From the smallest gnat to the largest aircraft, all things that fly obey the same aerodynamic principles. In The Simple Science of Flight, Henk Tennekes investigates just how machines and creatures fly: what size wings they need, how much energy is required for their journeys, how they cross deserts and oceans, how they take off, climb, and soar. Fascinated by the similarities between nature and technology, Tennekes offers an introduction to flight that teaches by association.\nL85: Swans and Boeings differ in numerous ways, but they follow the same aerodynamic principles. Biological evolution and its technical counterpart exhibit exciting parallels. What makes some airplanes successful and others misfits? Why does the Boeing 747 endure but the Concorde now seem a fluke? Tennekes explains the science of flight through comparisons, examples, equations, and anecdotes. The new edition of this popular book has been thoroughly revised and much expanded.\nL86: Highlights of the new material include a description of the incredible performance of bar-tailed godwits (7,000 miles nonstop from Alaska to New Zealand), an analysis of the convergence of modern jetliners (from both Boeing and Airbus), a discussion of the metabolization of energy featuring Lance Armstrong, a novel treatment of the aerodynamics of drag and trailing vortices, and an emphasis throughout on evolution, in nature and in engineering.\nL87: Tennekes draws on new evidence on bird migration, new wind-tunnel studies, and data on new airliners. And his analysis of the relative efficiency of planes, trains, and automobiles is newly relevant. (On a cost-per-seat scale, a 747 is more efficient than a passenger car.)\nL88: Henk Tennekes is Director of Research Emeritus at the Royal Netherlands Meteorological Institute, Emeritus Professor of Meteorology at the Free University (VU) in Amsterdam, and Emeritus Professor of Aerospace Engineering at Pennsylvania State University. He is the coauthor of A First Course in Turbulence (MIT Press, 1972).\nL89: ##### Additional Material\nL90: \nL91: 【17†Index†mitp-content-server.mit.edu】\nL92: \nL93: 【18†Preface†mitp-content-server.mit.edu】\nL94: \nL95: 【19†Sample Chapter†mitp-content-server.mit.edu】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGlobal Material Resources Outlook to 2060 (EN) (https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2019/02/global-material-resources-outlook-to-2060_g1g98d7d/9789264307452-en.pdf)\nciteturn245view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 214; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2019/02/global-material-resources-outlook-to-2060_g1g98d7d/9789264307452-en.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 6041\nL0@P0: Global Material Resources\nL1@P0: Outlook to 2060\nL2@P0: ECONOMIC DRIVERS AND ENVIRONMENTAL\nL3@P0-2: CONSEQUENCESGlobal Material\nL4@P2: Resources Outlook\nL5@P2: to 2060\nL6@P2: ECONOMIC DRIVERS AND ENVIRONMENTAL\nL7@P2-3: CONSEQUENCESThis work is published under the responsibility of the Secretary-General of the OECD. The\nL8@P3: opinions expressed and arguments employed herein do not necessarily reflect the official\nL9@P3: views of OECD member countries.\nL10@P3: This document, as well as any data and any map included herein, are without prejudice\nL11@P3: to the status of or sovereignty over any territory, to the delimitation of international\nL12@P3: frontiers and boundaries and to the name of any territory, city or area.\nL13@P3: Please cite this publication as:\nL14@P3: OECD (2019), Global Material Resources Outlook to 2060: Economic Drivers and Environmental\nL15@P3: Consequences, OECD Publishing, Paris.\nL16@P3: https://doi.org/10.1787/9789264307452-en\nL17@P3: ISBN 978-92-64-30744-5 (print)\nL18@P3: ISBN 978-92-64-30745-2 (pdf)\nL19@P3: ISBN 978-92-64-32828-0 (HTML)\nL20@P3: ISBN 978-92-64-39828-3 (epub)\nL21@P3: The statistical data for Israel are supplied by and under the responsibility of the relevant Israeli authorities. The use of\nL22@P3: such data by the OECD is without prejudice to the status of the Golan Heights, East Jerusalem and Israeli settlements in\nL23@P3: the West Bank under the terms of international law.\nL24@P3: Photo credits: Cover © DR Travel Photo and Video/Shutterstock.com\nL25@P3: Corrigenda to OECD publications may be found on line at: www.oecd.org/about/publishing/corrigenda.htm.\nL26@P3: © OECD 2019\nL27@P3: You can copy, download or print OECD content for your own use, and you can include excerpts from OECD publications, databases and\nL28@P3: multimedia products in your own documents, presentations, blogs, websites and teaching materials, provided that suitable\nL29@P3: acknowledgement of OECD as source and copyright owner is given. All requests for public or commercial use and translation rights should\nL30@P3: be submitted to rights@oecd.org. Requests for permission to photocopy portions of this material for public or commercial use shall be\nL31@P3: addressed directly to the Copyright Clearance Center (CCC) at info@copyright.com or the Centre français d’exploitation du droit de copie\nL32@P3-4: (CFC) at contact@cfcopies.com.FOREWORD │ 3\nL33@P4: GLOBAL MATERIAL RESOURCES OUTLOOK TO 2060 © OECD 2019\nL34@P4: Foreword\nL35@P4: As nations become wealthier, does this necessarily lead to a proportional increase in the\nL36@P4: weight of the materials they consume? Can economies successfully decouple materials\nL37@P4: use from economic growth? These pressing questions lie at the heart of national and\nL38@P4: international discussions about the transition to a more resource efficient, circular\nL39@P4: economy.\nL40@P4: Globally, the use of material resources grew from 27 billion tonnes (Gt) in 1970 to\nL41@P4: 89 billion tonnes in 2017. This will increase further with continued population growth\nL42@P4: and economic development. However, such growth in materials use, coupled with the\nL43@P4: environmental consequences of material extraction, processing and waste, is likely to\nL44@P4: increase the pressure on the resource bases of our economies and jeopardise future gains\nL45@P4: in well-being.\nL46@P4: Many OECD countries and emerging economies are implementing policies to stimulate\nL47@P4: the transition to circular economies. Resource efficiency is also central to our efforts to\nL48@P4: deliver on the Paris Agreement and achieve the Sustainable Development Goals,\nL49@P4: especially those related to climate change, biodiversity, water, energy and responsible\nL50@P4: consumption and production. In addition, global fora like the G7 and the G20 are\nL51@P4: increasingly prioritising resource efficiency in their discussions.\nL52@P4: The Global Material Resources Outlook to 2060 seeks to support these efforts through\nL53@P4: projections on the future use of material resources. It is the first report that provides an in\u0002depth examination of the likely path of future materials use and the economic drivers that\nL54@P4: underpin these projections. The report focuses on three major socioeconomic trends that\nL55@P4: influence the use of material resources: the expected growth in emerging and developing\nL56@P4: economies relying on material-intensive infrastructure development; the evolution of\nL57@P4: many economies towards a greater role for the services sector; and the accelerating\nL58@P4: transformation of production technologies and processes. These trends will have\nL59@P4: differing, and sometimes conflicting, impacts on the scale and intensity of future\nL60@P4: materials use.\nL61@P4: This Outlook projects that, in the absence of new policies, global materials use would rise\nL62@P4: from 89 Gt in 2017 to 167 Gt in 2060. This growth is reflected in all major categories of\nL63@P4: materials: metallic ores (9 to 20 Gt), non-metallic minerals (44 to 86 Gt), biomass (22 to\nL64@P4: 37 Gt) and fossil fuels (15 to 24 Gt). In addition, the extraction, processing and disposal\nL65@P4: of materials brings significant environmental consequences, which will be magnified as\nL66@P4: materials use increases. These include a doubling of greenhouse gas emissions, pollution\nL67@P4: to the soil, water and air, and toxic effects on humans and aquatic and terrestrial\nL68@P4-5: ecosystems. Such environmental consequences will hurt our economies and our societies. 4 │ FOREWORD\nL69@P5: GLOBAL MATERIAL RESOURCES OUTLOOK TO 2060 © OECD 2019\nL70@P5: \nL71@P5: This Outlook can help decision-makers understand the direction in which we are heading\nL72@P5: and help to assess which policies can support a more circular economy. The OECD stands\nL73@P5: ready to assist governments in making this transition by designing, developing and\nL74@P5: delivering better resource efficiency policies for better lives.\nL75@P5: Angel Gurría\nL76@P5-6: OECD Secretary-General ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS │ 5\nL77@P6: GLOBAL MATERIAL RESOURCES OUTLOOK TO 2060 © OECD 2019\nL78@P6: Acknowledgements\nL79@P6: This report was managed by Shardul Agrawala, Head of the Environment and Economy\nL80@P6: Integration Division of the OECD Environment Directorate. Ruben Bibas, Jean Chateau,\nL81@P6: Rob Dellink and Elisa Lanzi of the OECD Environment Directorate prepared the report\nL82@P6: and carried out the underlying modelling analysis. Martin Benkovic provided modelling\nL83@P6: support and created the graphics in the report. Peter Börkey provided guidance on the\nL84@P6: policy aspects of resource efficiency and the transition to a circular economy.\nL85@P6: Stefan Giljum and Mirko Lieber (Vienna University, Austria) provided the material flow\nL86@P6: data. Jinxue Hu and Ton Bastein (TNO Research, The Netherlands) provided data on\nL87@P6: recycling and reprocessing. Frank Pothen (Leibniz Universität Hannover, Germany)\nL88@P6: provided information on materials decoupling. Lisa Congyuan Yao and Silpa Kaza\nL89@P6: (World Bank) provided the analysis on municipal waste generation. Alvaro Calzadilla,\nL90@P6: Victor Nechifor and Matthew Winning (University College London, United Kingdom)\nL91@P6: provided the case study analysis on iron and steel. Matthias Pfaff and Marcel Soulier\nL92@P6: (Fraunhofer ISI, Germany) provided the case study analysis on copper. Lauran van Oers\nL93@P6: and Ester van der Voet (Leiden University, The Netherlands) provided the environmental\nL94@P6: impact assessment on metals and construction materials.\nL95@P6: Katjusha Boffa, Aziza Perrière and Jack McNeill (all OECD Environment Directorate)\nL96@P6: provided administrative support. Beth del Bourgo, Jessica Li and Tobias Udsholt (all\nL97@P6: OECD Environment Directorate) created communication materials for the report. The\nL98@P6: report was edited by Fiona Hinchcliffe with guidance from Janine Treves (OECD Public\nL99@P6: Affairs and Communications Directorate).\nL100@P6: The OECD Environment Policy Committee (EPOC) was responsible for the oversight of\nL101@P6: the development of the report. In addition, the Working Party on Resource Productivity\nL102@P6: and Waste (WPRPW) and the Working Party on Integrating Environmental and\nL103@P6: Economic Policies (WPIEEP) and the experts that participated in the RE-CIRCLE\nL104@P6: Technical Workshops reviewed earlier drafts.\nL105@P6: The report also benefited from expert input and feedback on earlier drafts by Elisabetta\nL106@P6: Cornago, Anthony Cox, Frithjof Laubinger, Myriam Linster, and Andrew McCarthy (all\nL107@P6: OECD Environment Directorate), Araceli Fernandez Pales (International Energy Agency)\nL108@P6: as well as Raimund Bleischwitz (University College London, United Kingdom), Tara\nL109@P6: Caetano (University of Cape Town, South Africa), Martin Distelkamp (GWS, Germany),\nL110@P6: Stefan Giljum (Vienna University of Economics and Business, Austria) and Steve\nL111@P6: Hatfield-Dodds (ABARES, Australia).\nL112@P6: Finally, this work would not have been possible without the financial support from\nL113@P6-8: France, Luxembourg, the Netherlands, Switzerland and the United Kingdom.TABLE OF CONTENTS │ 7\nL114@P8: GLOBAL MATERIAL RESOURCES OUTLOOK TO 2060 © OECD 2019\nL115@P8: Table of contents\nL116@P8: Foreword ................................................................................................................................................ 3\nL117@P8: Acknowledgements ................................................................................................................................ 5\nL118@P8: Abbreviations and acronyms .............................................................................................................. 13\nL119@P8: Executive summary ............................................................................................................................. 15\nL120@P8: What are the key projected trends for materials use? ........................................................................ 15\n"
